**2. In tests**, use `FakeTimeProvider` (from `Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing`):

```csharp
var fake = new FakeTimeProvider(new DateTimeOffset(2026, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero));
var calc = new InvoiceCalculator(fake);

Assert.True(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2025, 12, 31, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));

fake.Advance(TimeSpan.FromDays(-365));
Assert.False(calc.IsOverdue(new DateTimeOffset(2025, 12, 31, 0, 0, 0, TimeSpan.Zero)));
```

**Why this is the lightest option:**

- **No DI container** — the `? = null` default means `new InvoiceCalculator()` still works unchanged.
- **No custom interfaces** — `TimeProvider` is in `System` (BCL), zero production dependencies.
- **One test package** — add `Microsoft.Extensions.Time.Testing` to your test project only.
- **Fully controllable** — `FakeTimeProvider` lets you set and advance time deterministically.
